# HTTP Proxies

HTTP proxies are commonly used for web traffic.

A simplified architecture is:

```text
Browser
   ↓
HTTP Proxy
   ↓
Website
```

They can be useful for applications where HTTP/HTTPS traffic is sufficient.

For browser automation, compatibility depends on the browser and automation environment.

---

# SOCKS5 Proxies

SOCKS5 operates at a lower level than application-specific HTTP proxying.

Conceptually:

```text
Browser
   ↓
SOCKS5 Proxy
   ↓
Destination
```

SOCKS5 can be useful when an application needs broader network routing capabilities.

See:

[SOCKS5 Proxy](../proxy/socks5-proxy.md)

---

# Residential Proxies

Residential proxies route traffic through IP addresses associated with residential networks.

They are commonly used for:

* Geographic testing
* Market research
* Localization
* Web testing

Residential does not automatically mean "undetectable."

Website risk systems can evaluate many signals beyond IP type.

See:

[Residential Proxy](../proxy/residential-proxy.md)

---

# Mobile Proxies

Mobile proxies use IP addresses associated with mobile networks.

They can be useful when testing mobile-network scenarios or regional experiences.

Again, proxy type is only one component of the overall browser environment.

See:

[Mobile Proxy](../proxy/mobile-proxy.md)

