# Proxy vs Browser Fingerprint

This is one of the most important concepts in browser infrastructure.

A proxy primarily changes the **network path**.

A browser fingerprint describes the **browser environment**.

For example:

```text
Proxy
└── Network address

Fingerprint
├── Browser
├── Operating system
├── Screen
├── Canvas
├── WebGL
├── Audio
├── Fonts
└── Other browser signals
```

Changing one does not automatically change the other.

---

# Why IP Address Alone Is Not Enough

A website can potentially observe more than the network address.

A simplified example:

```text
Network
 └── IP address

Browser
 ├── User agent
 ├── Screen
 ├── WebGL
 ├── Canvas
 ├── Audio
 ├── Fonts
 └── JavaScript behavior

Session
 ├── Cookies
 └── Local storage
```

These layers can interact.

Therefore:

> A proxy is not a replacement for browser-profile management.

Likewise:

> A browser fingerprint is not a replacement for appropriate network configuration.

---

# AI Agents Need Stable Network Configuration

An AI agent may operate through a long sequence:

```text
Open
 ↓
Login
 ↓
Navigate
 ↓
Search
 ↓
Read
 ↓
Submit
 ↓
Verify
```

If the network environment changes unexpectedly during the workflow, troubleshooting becomes harder.

For many legitimate workflows, stable network configuration is easier to manage.

A profile can therefore be associated with a specific network configuration:

```text
Profile A
 ├── Browser Environment A
 └── Proxy A

Profile B
 ├── Browser Environment B
 └── Proxy B
```

This creates a clearer relationship between browser state and network state.

---

# Proxy Rotation

Proxy rotation means changing the proxy used by a workflow.

A simplified example:

```text
Request 1 → Proxy A
Request 2 → Proxy B
Request 3 → Proxy C
```

Rotation can be useful for certain testing and research architectures.

However, unnecessary rotation can make sessions harder to manage.

For a persistent browser workflow, the question is not simply:

> "How often should the proxy change?"

A better question is:

> "Does this workflow actually require the network identity to change?"

---

# Sticky Proxies

A sticky proxy maintains the same proxy for a period of time.

For example:

```text
Agent
 ↓
Proxy A
 ↓
Requests 1–20
```

This can be easier to manage when a workflow needs a relatively stable network environment.

Sticky behavior depends on the proxy provider and configuration.

---

# Proxy Rotation vs Sticky Sessions

| Model           | Description                        | Typical Use                  |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Rotating proxy  | Address changes periodically       | Distributed research/testing |
| Sticky proxy    | Address remains stable temporarily | Persistent workflows         |
| Dedicated proxy | Assigned to one environment        | Controlled testing           |
| Shared proxy    | Used by multiple users             | General routing              |

There is no universally best proxy model.

The correct choice depends on the application.

