# Proxy Management at Scale

When the number of browser sessions increases, manually managing proxies becomes difficult.

A proxy management system may track:

* Proxy address
* Port
* Protocol
* Geographic location
* Authentication
* Availability
* Current assignment
* Session status
* Rotation status

A simplified database could look like:

```text
Proxy ID | Location | Status | Assigned Profile
------------------------------------------------
P001     | US       | Active | Profile A
P002     | UK       | Active | Profile B
P003     | DE       | Idle   | -
```

This allows an automation controller to make better allocation decisions.

---

# Proxy Health

A proxy should not be treated as permanently reliable.

A useful proxy-management system can monitor:

* Connection success
* Response time
* Timeouts
* Authentication failures
* Availability
* Geographic consistency

For example:

```text
Proxy A
Latency: Low
Status: Healthy

Proxy B
Latency: High
Status: Warning

Proxy C
Connection failed
Status: Offline
```

An AI agent should ideally receive a healthy network resource rather than blindly selecting an unavailable proxy.

---

# AI Agents and Proxy Failure

Network failures are normal in distributed browser automation.

A workflow may encounter:

```text
Agent
 ↓
Proxy
 ↓
Connection Failed
```

A robust system can respond:

```text
Detect failure
     ↓
Check proxy
     ↓
Retry connection
     ↓
Use approved fallback
     ↓
Resume task
```

The fallback policy should be determined by the application.

For some workflows, changing the network environment during a session may be undesirable.

For others, retrying with another network can be appropriate.

---

# Proxy Authentication

Many proxies require authentication.

Common models include:

```text
Username + Password
```

or provider-specific authentication methods.

Credentials should be stored securely.

Avoid placing proxy credentials directly inside source code:

```text
BAD:
proxy = "username:password@host:port"
```

Instead, use secure configuration or environment variables where appropriate.

---

# Proxy Credentials and AI Agents

AI agents introduce another security consideration.

An AI model should not automatically have unrestricted access to proxy credentials.

A safer architecture is:

```text
AI Agent
   ↓
Approved Tool
   ↓
Proxy Manager
   ↓
Authenticated Proxy
```

The agent requests a network resource.

The infrastructure handles the credentials.

This separates reasoning from sensitive infrastructure secrets.

---

# AI Agents, Proxies, and MCP

MCP can provide a tool layer between an AI agent and browser infrastructure.

A conceptual system could look like:

```text
AI Model
    ↓
AI Agent
    ↓
MCP
    ├── Browser Tool
    ├── Profile Tool
    └── Network Tool
            ↓
        Proxy Manager
            ↓
          Proxy
            ↓
         Website
```

The AI does not necessarily need direct access to the underlying proxy credentials.

Instead, tools can expose controlled operations.

For example:

```text
get_available_network()
assign_network(profile)
check_network_status()
release_network(profile)
```

The exact implementation depends on the automation platform.

